1
00:00:04,003 --> 00:00:08,959
In this tutorial you will describe an Arduino Uno board in a DACQ (data acquisition) configuration.

2
00:00:09,009 --> 00:00:14,432
We continue with the MotorLearning experiment created in the previous tutorial, its subjects and sessions.

3
00:00:14,482 --> 00:00:25,136
Select the experiment and click the 'DACQ Configuration' button.

4
00:00:25,186 --> 00:00:31,495
Choose the system: ADwin or Arduino UNO.

5
00:00:31,545 --> 00:00:38,458
As default configuration of the experiment, it will be pre-selected when processes are created.

6
00:00:38,508 --> 00:00:45,962
The configuration appears in the Experiments view with its system. Double click to open its editor.

7
00:00:46,012 --> 00:00:50,935
Double click on the editor tab to maximize it.

8
00:00:50,985 --> 00:00:56,595
The General tab describes how DOCoMETRe compiles the processes and reaches the board.

9
00:00:56,645 --> 00:01:01,505
Arduino libraries are provided with DOCoMETRe. Check 'Use command line tool' to compile and upload with arduino-cli...

10
00:01:01,555 --> 00:01:06,496
...and give its path (Browse... opens a file dialog).

11
00:01:06,546 --> 00:01:12,805
Choose the revision of your board: R3 or R4 WiFi.

12
00:01:12,855 --> 00:01:19,634
The serial port of the board: COM3 on Windows, /dev/ttyACM0 on Linux... Browse... lists the available ports.

13
00:01:19,684 --> 00:01:28,335
The global frequency is the frequency of the real time loop. Click on its value to change it.

14
00:01:28,385 --> 00:01:37,168
Each module of the board has its own tab: analog inputs, analog outputs and digital inputs/outputs.

15
00:01:37,218 --> 00:01:47,981
Analog inputs tab: first choose the analog reference. AR_DEFAULT (5 V) is the default one of the R4 WiFi.

16
00:01:48,031 --> 00:01:52,374
Then check the channels you use. Here a force sensor is wired on pin A1.

17
00:01:52,424 --> 00:01:56,787
T (Transfer): values are sent to the computer, to be displayed during acquisition.

18
00:01:56,837 --> 00:02:01,225
Rec. (Record): transferred values are saved in a samples file for each trial.

19
00:02:01,275 --> 00:02:07,801
Give the channel a meaningful name: it is used in processes, charts and data files names.

20
00:02:07,851 --> 00:02:18,521
S.F. is the sample frequency of the channel, a sub-multiple of the global frequency: 500 Hz here.

21
00:02:18,571 --> 00:02:33,767
Digital inputs/outputs tab: pin 13 (the LED of the board) is used as a digital output.

22
00:02:33,817 --> 00:02:41,086
Variables tab: variables are used by the processes (counters, states, computed values...).

23
00:02:41,136 --> 00:02:52,884
Add a variable, give it a name and a type.

24
00:02:52,934 --> 00:02:59,302
Checked as Parameter, its value will be read for each trial in the parameters file of the session.

25
00:02:59,352 --> 00:03:06,710
Charts tab: the charts displayed in real time during acquisition.

26
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:11,644
Several types are available: oscilloscope, XY, gauge, tank, image... Here an oscilloscope chart.

27
00:03:11,694 --> 00:03:21,303
Add the curves to display: only transferred channels and variables are listed.

28
00:03:21,353 --> 00:03:29,176
Set the properties of the chart: a 5 seconds time width, with automatic scaling.

29
00:03:29,226 --> 00:03:34,970
Save the configuration with Ctrl+S.

30
00:03:35,020 --> 00:03:46,660
This button saves these settings in the preferences: they will initialize your next Arduino configurations.

31
00:03:46,710 --> 00:03:51,667
The DACQ configuration is ready. Next tutorial: create a process that uses these channels and variables.

32
00:03:51,717 --> 00:03:57,967
Quit DOCoMETRe with File > Exit DOCoMETRe.

